WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
This terminal shows a problem common in react native development,

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.500
adjust test suite crashing with a type error on Get Enforcing

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.700
before a single assertion runs.

4
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:14.500
Just runs in node, lacking the native device runtime.

5
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.000
Because of this, the connection breaks.

6
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.000
This load time crash occurs the moment to file imports and calls the registry.

7
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.700
If it fails, the suite stops before reaching your tests.

8
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.900
To fix this, we need to intercept these imports using Mox.

9
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.600
This allows us to bypass the missing runtime and reach a passing state.

10
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.400
Open your project in your code editor so you can implement these strategies as we go.

11
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.400
Without bridging this runtime gap at the point of import,

12
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.600
the test suite remains unable to load the components you need to test.

13
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.100
Strategy 1 is the manual mock.

14
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:54.400
This is the direct approach when you want to isolate a single module and its specific dependencies.

15
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.500
In your editor, find the directory containing your turbo module spec.

16
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.900
Create a folder there named underscore underscore mox underscore underscore.

17
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:07.000
Inside that folder, create a file mirroring your spec's name.

18
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.500
From this file, export a dummy object to act as the placeholder.

19
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:16.100
Just module system will now see this file and use it to satisfy the import.

20
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.200
This prevents the actual native registry from being called during the test.

21
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:25.300
If you run your tests now, the Get Enforcing error for this module should be resolved.

22
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:30.200
Localized mocking is a precise way to neutralize isolated native dependencies

23
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.200
without changing how the rest of the application tests.

24
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.800
While manual mox work for single files, projects with dozens of native modules face a maintenance burden

25
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.200
if every spec requires its own folder.

26
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.100
Strategy 2 uses a global registry mock to automate this.

27
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.600
Start by creating a just.setup.ts file in your project route.

28
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.100
In this file, mock the turbo module registry directly.

29
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:59.800
This ensures that any call to Get Enforcing across the entire app receives a fallback mock object automatically.

30
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:04.600
Inside this mock, include an error message like module is not registered.

31
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:09.800
This alerts you immediately if a new module is added without its specific implementation.

32
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:17.500
To finish the setup, link this file to your just.config.js by adding its path to the setup files array.

33
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:21.500
Using a global setup file ensures that as your project grows,

34
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:25.800
every native module is accounted for in a single central location.

35
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.500
We must also consider snapshot tests.

36
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:33.600
Native modules return device-specific data, like screen dimensions or time zones.

37
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:39.800
These dynamic values cause snapshot tests to fail whenever they are run on a machine with different local settings.

38
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.600
To prevent this, hard code static values into your mox.

39
00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:51.400
Setting a property like screen width to a fixed number like 1080 ensures the snapshot remains identical across all environments.

40
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.300
This matrix framework helps choose your strategy.

41
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.500
Use strategy 1 or 2 for basic mocking.

42
00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:01.300
For top-level registry calls, use strategy 3's factory mock.

43
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:06.700
For business logic, strategy 4's service abstraction wraps the native module completely.

44
00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:13.700
Handling turbo module tests is about choosing the abstraction layer that matches the scale and logic requirements of your code base.

45
00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:17.200
Now run your test command in the terminal to verify the changes.

46
00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.300
The terminal now shows a clean run.

47
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.100
The load time crashes are gone, replaced by passing indicators.

48
00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:30.600
By intercepting those native imports, you have created a reliable testing environment that works within the limitations of the node run time.

49
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.900
To learn more, consult the official JustGuide for React Native

50
00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:38.400
or examine the real-world turbo module tests in the Async storage repository.

